a.notes:
Through this lens the heat's best news is Lit Sill Line. Its phosphor is no longer an effect placed on a page: the light IS the page content.
- On Home, 'now' is a breathing lamp, the run to the next plan is lit, and the tube's power lamp drops a wire to the rail.
- On Status, the tube's glass carries the lamp tally.
- Everything else is slate, steel and midnight ink.
That is the owner's 'bright green where it needs to be, grounded elsewhere' made structural, and it should become the lineage's standard.

Grafted features that worked:
- the greeting standing on the Coming up rail, with the tube's lit drop wire (track-graft);
- airglow off the lit run as the one divider (track-graft; lessons asked for exactly this);
- the Status tube carrying the lamp tally in VT323 (track-graft);
- the midnight-ink family plates (track-graft, L004);
- the board's line-of-light refresh on power-on (timetable-graft);
- sunk readout windows with glowing 'Working' lamps (timetable-graft, L034);
- the lit today figure and weekend bands on the glass (pocket-graft);
- the lamp key in every footer (pocket-graft), a real system element.

Grafts that did not work:
- timetable-graft's enamel interchange stubs and names: other-colour neon rose to 14.7%, the only design here not grounded;
- pocket-graft's large amber head figure on Status;
- track-graft's month still opening on folded past weeks.

The dice mutants taught one thing each:
- track-mut: engraved condensed legends suit a signal panel, but type alone adds no light;
- timetable-mut: steel drafting outside the glass is drawing, not phosphor; a scale should be lit inside the tube;
- pocket-mut: a 5:1 panorama tube is not a period screen.

The kit effect still missing almost everywhere is row afterglow. In the details strips only the timetable entrants, and pocket-refine faintly, show a green edge at 120ms; the track entrants' steel washes do not read. Power-on and focus are faithful in all sixteen, and the timetable raster fill and the track-graft sweep are the best power-ons.

On the owner's 'reflect more of the page content': the carried designs and mutants changed least. The changes that reach page structure are the Graft's greeting-on-the-line, the Refine's tube as the first stop, and the Diary's date rows, and those are the ones this lens rewards.

fixes.suggestion:
KEEP:
- the greeting standing on the Coming up rail;
- the lit run from 'now' to the tube and the power lamp's glowing drop wire;
- the airglow rising off the lit run (one per page);
- 'planning' in the E1 halo;
- the Status spend tube carrying the lamp tally ('WHO ANSWERS ●●●○ 3 OF 4') on its glass, and the big bone tally figures;
- midnight-ink plates for what the family wrote.
FIX:
(1) Row afterglow is still invisible in every still. Give a pointed row a 2px sage/phosphor left edge plus a faint inner wash that fades on the kit's 1.2s curve, visible at 120 and 380ms.
(2) Status: about 160px of dead slate sits right of the tally. Put the tally beside the tube under one bezel, or widen the tube so the tally lines fill it.
(3) Coming up plates wrap names onto two lines at 1280. Let the rail run the full board width, with the tube over the first stop and five plates in a row, and stand 'All plans' at the buffer.
(4) The month opens on four folded past weeks. Open it on the week holding today and show one count ('5 coming').
(5) Chat: light only the newest stop, with her screen and time aglow, and keep older bubble edges matte.
(6) Phone: trim the tube's foot band, which leaves 40px of empty glass above the prompt.

KEEP:
- the tube hung from Coming up's first stop;
- the Ideas line from home, with its stations named in blooming VT323;
- the Status one-bezel instrument with a labelled lamp board;
- the lamp legend under it;
- 'your turn' lit at the chat's terminus;
- terracotta, moss and wheat kind rings.
FIX:
(1) The top of Home lost phosphor when the tube moved down. Put E7's lit-from-below pool and its masked scanlines back behind the box, stronger than now, and give the box its resting halo (E4), so the question is lit before the line is.
(2) The other four plates are much shorter than the tube, which leaves slate under them. Give each plate a small inset readout (a date and time well) or let the tube shrink to 4:3 at plate height.
(3) Afterglow: the steel wash doesn't read. Use a sage edge fading over 1.2s.
(4) Take the Graft's airglow off the lit run from 'now' as the one divider on Home.

KEEP:
- the departure board with phosphor leaders and its raster power-on;
- Vera's line with a glowing terminus;
- the posted dotted leaders on every list;
- the enamel roundels;
- 'plan' in the halo;
- the visible green afterglow edge.
FIX:
(1) Chat is about 96% green. Keep Vera's newest stop lit, draw older bubble edges in pewter, and give the family's bubbles a grounded solid (bone or steel-denim fill), not only a thin edge.
(2) The board outshines the box at a squint. Raise the box's resting pool (E4 plus E7 scanlines inside the box plate) so the question and the board balance.
(3) Graft in the Interchange's refresh line on the board's power-on and Status's sunk readout windows with lit 'Working' lamps.
(4) The month's busy week has 30px of empty slate. Size the row to its content.

KEEP:
